      {
        "id": 213308,
        "name": "GreatDebate",
        "username": "GreatDebate",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/g/f08c70/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2018-02-14T02:35:55.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"Phantom5613\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/phantom5613/48/1906_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Phantom5613:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>'Defend' reduces the ammount of damage taken until your next action.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>Are you sure? &nbsp;The book says pretty clearly that it applies to the NEXT time you would take damage. &nbsp;The statement about it lasting until your next turn is only there to let you know that it expires by the beginning of your next turn.</p>\n<p>Still, I hope you're right - because I think it makes Defend more useful when it persists. Plus, it's more comic-book-ish because often a Speedster or agile character is capable of avoiding most damage throughout an entire scene. &nbsp;(But I don't think that's the actual rule here.)</p>\n<p>I think it would work nicely if you kept track of your Defend result and then used it up as you took damage. So, if you roll a 7 effect, you could dodge your first 7 points of damage until the next turn.</p>\n<p>Just my opinion though.</p>",
        "post_number": 3,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T05:44:15.373Z",
        "reply_count": 0,
        "reply_to_post_number": null,
        "quote_count": 0,
        "incoming_link_count": 0,
        "reads": 0,
        "readers_count": 0,
        "score": 0.0,
        "yours": false,
        "topic_id": 17688,
        "topic_slug": "defend-question",
        "display_username": "GreatDebate",
        "primary_group_name": null,
        "flair_name": null,
        "flair_url": null,
        "flair_bg_color": null,
        "flair_color": null,
        "flair_group_id": null,
        "badges_granted": [],
        "version": 1,
        "can_edit": false,
        "can_delete": false,
        "can_recover": false,
        "can_see_hidden_post": false,
        "can_wiki": false,
        "read": false,
        "user_title": null,
        "bookmarked": false,
        "raw": "[quote=\"Phantom5613\"]\n\n&#39;Defend&#39; reduces the ammount of damage taken until your next action.\n\n[/quote]\n\n&nbsp;\n\nAre you sure? &nbsp;The book says pretty clearly that it applies to the NEXT time you would take damage. &nbsp;The statement about it lasting until your next turn is only there to let you know that it expires by the beginning of your next turn.\n\nStill, I hope you&#39;re right - because I think it makes Defend more useful when it persists. Plus, it&#39;s more comic-book-ish because often a Speedster or agile character is capable of avoiding most damage throughout an entire scene. &nbsp;(But I don&#39;t think that&#39;s the actual rule here.)\n\nI think it would work nicely if you kept track of your Defend result and then used it up as you took damage. So, if you roll a 7 effect, you could dodge your first 7 points of damage until the next turn.\n\nJust my opinion though.\n",
